00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:03.830 align:start position:0%
today marks the 50th anniversary of the  ibrox disaster  66 football fans perished as the result  of a crush  at stairway 13. a solemn act of  remembrance  in an empty stadium the old firm rivalry  is a bitter one  and deep rooted but it's always been put  aside to remember the 66 lives that were  lost here at ibrox  50 years ago the current rangers manager  stephen gerrard lost his cousin at  hillsborough so  understands better than most the  significance of the anniversary  because of the call with 19 things will  look slightly different  but you know as a club we still want to  pay our full respect to everyone that  was involved  um and so all the names that are no  longer with us  certainly know what this  disaster means to people and obviously  want to send my full respect to  all the families and the survivors and  also pay my respects to the people that  passed  65 men and boys 30 of them just  teenagers and one  18 year old girl died in the crush on  stairway 13  at ibrox 200 more were injured  the sum of the 80 000 fans began to  leave the ground following a 90th minute  celtic goal  that thought someone fell causing a  pileup of bodies that in places was six  deep the survivors some of whom tried to  help the rescue efforts  were left in a state of shock before you  knew it  because of the volume of pressure on  your body  you know you couldn't show you the air  was getting squeezed out your lungs  and they you know that you felt as if  everything was going to pack in you and  you couldn't shout  my legs were wrapped around a boy behind  me and i was looking off my arm  was still in a position where i could  move them  and at one stage i thought that was it  that i was gone and they  actually prayed i said please god don't  let me die  the club had planned to mark today's  anniversary with a fitting commemoration  but kovid restrictions forbid it a few  socially distanced supporters  the only ones able to pay their respects  ranges one  celtic nil unlike the old firm clash of  50 years ago  today's ended in a win for rangers but  for many today is not about goals  points or bragging rights it's about  remembering 66 people  who came here to enjoy a game of  football but who never came home  fraser moored sky news
 

